@id     05_pointers
@title  Pointers
@topic  pointers
@lang   c

@theory
A pointer stores the address of another variable.

    int x = 5;
    int *p = &x;   // p points to x
    *p = 10;       // changes x through the pointer, now x == 10

Functions in C get copies of their arguments. To let a function change
the caller's variables, pass their addresses:

    void set_to_zero(int *v) { *v = 0; }
    set_to_zero(&x);

Your task: write swap so that it exchanges the values of two variables.
The program reads a and b and prints them after the swap.

Input:   3 5
Output:  5 3

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    // exchange the values that a and b point to
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
3 5
@expected
5 3

@test
@input
-1 0
@expected
0 -1

@hidden_test
@input
7 7
@expected
7 7

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step explore
@title  Where does the arrow go?
@scene  pointer_basics
@text
Press the buttons and watch the arrow. p = &a stores the address of a in p.
*p = 5 changes whatever p points at. Try *p = 5 while p is NULL.

@step watch
@title  Watch swap
@code
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 5;
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@text
Step through the program. On line 11 press "Step into" to enter swap,
then watch where a and b point and how x and y change.

@step predict
@title  Predict a value
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3;
    int *p = &x;
    *p = 10;
    printf("%d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
@line   6
@expr   x
@text
What is the value of x after line 6 runs?

@step predict
@title  Predict the target
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 7;
    int *p = &x;
    p = &y;
    *p = 1;
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@line   6
@expr   p
@text
After line 6 runs, which variable does p point at?

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 7;
    int *p = &x;
    p = &y;
    *p = 1;
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@text
What does this program print?

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
This swap does not swap anything. Use the debugger to see why, then fix it.
The program reads two numbers and should print them swapped.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int a, int b) {
    int t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(x, y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@test
@input
3 5
@expected
5 3
@hidden_test
@input
-1 8
@expected
8 -1

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
Why does swap need pointers? Why doesn't swap(int a, int b) work?
@rubric
copy; address; caller's variables
@answer
C passes arguments by value: swap(int a, int b) gets copies of x and y, so changing
the copies does not change the caller's variables. Passing &x gives swap the address
of x, so *a = ... writes to x itself.

@step practice
